course: dictionary
lesson: 10197
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the words around them show which meaning is meant. The word tin is a good example. «Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin; it is harder than either metal» shows tin as a metal, the soft, silvery-white stuff.
explain: The same word can name a container. «Biscuits kept in a tin» would mean a metal box with a lid, because the words biscuits and kept tell you what a tin is for. Ask yourself: what meaning would fit here? Then try that meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: In the UK and Australia, a tin can also be a sealed metal can of food, like a tin of beans. Americans would say «can», so tin is a regional word too. Tin can also describe something made of thin metal, like a tin roof.
fact: Tin is a soft, silvery-white metal.
fact: A tin can be a metal container with a lid, for keeping biscuits or cake.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, a tin is a sealed metal can of food; Americans say «can».
fact: Mixing tin with copper makes bronze, and people learned to do this at the start of the Bronze Age.
fact: «Tin» can also describe something made of thin metal, as in «a tin roof».
exercise: «Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin.» What is tin in this sentence? | *A soft, silvery-white metal | A thin metal roof | A can of beans | A cake tin with a lid
exercise: Mum says, «Put the cake in the tin.» Which meaning fits best? | A sealed can of food from the shop | A soft metal used in bronze | Something made of thin metal | *A metal container with a lid, for keeping cake
exercise: In Australia, Ella says, «Grab a tin of peaches.» What does tin mean? | A metal box for biscuits | A roof made of thin metal | A silvery-white metal | *A sealed metal can of food
test: «The tin roof sparkled in the sun.» What does tin mean in this sentence? | A cake container with a lid | A soft, silvery-white metal | A tin of food | *Made of thin metal
test: Why would an Australian say «tin» where an American says «can»? | *Because tin is a regional word for a sealed metal can of food | Because tin and can mean the same metal | Because tin is a kind of bread | Because cans are made of wood
exam: Which sentence shows tin as a metal rather than a container? | «Biscuits are kept in a tin with a lid.» | «The tin roof was old and rusty.» | *«Bronze is an alloy of copper and tin.» | «He opened a tin of beans for lunch.»
